    citation: "2023 INSC 1017 (3-Judge Bench) & (2017) 3 SCC 1 (7-Judge Bench)",
    passageText: `In a parliamentary democracy, the power to enact legislation is entrusted to the elected representatives of the people in the Legislature. When a Bill has been passed by the Legislative Assembly of a State (or by both Houses in a bicameral State) and is presented to the Governor under the substantive part of Article 200 of the Constitution, the Governor is presented with three constitutional options: (i) he declares that he assents to the Bill; (ii) he declares that he withholds assent therefrom; or (iii) he declares that he reserves the Bill for the consideration of the President. Crucially, the first proviso to Article 200—which opens with the words 'Provided that the Governor may, as soon as possible after the presentation to him of the Bill for assent, return the Bill if it is not a Money Bill together with a message requesting that the House or Houses will reconsider the Bill'—is NOT an independent fourth option detached from withholding assent; rather, it is attached to and qualifies the option of withholding assent. If an unelected Governor were permitted to simply declare that he 'withholds assent' and sit indefinitely on Bills passed by the elected Legislature without returning them to the House under the first proviso, it would vest the Governor with a 'pocket veto' alien to the constitutional design.

A parallel constitutional check applies to the Executive's legislative power to promulgate Ordinances under Articles 123 and 213 when the Legislature is not in session. As authoritatively settled by a seven-Judge Constitution Bench in [1], the power to promulgate an Ordinance is a conditional measure of emergent necessity, not a parallel source of legislation; failure to lay an Ordinance before the Legislature, or the practice of 're-promulgating' Ordinances repeatedly without placing them before the House, is a 'fraud on the Constitution'.`,
